// @emit-error emitProgram: if-expression arm ends in a statement, which yields no value
// @warning Unused variable `z` (prefix with `_` to suppress, or remove it)
// BOUNDARY PIN (message wording, not coverage): the second of the three ways an
// if-expression arm can fail to produce a value. Here the arm's last statement is a real
// STATEMENT — a `let`/`const` declaration — which leaves nothing on the stack. The sibling
// cause, a void CALL, is pinned by `error-if-arm-ends-in-void-call.vl`; an EMPTY arm block
// is pinned by `error-if-arm-is-empty.vl`. All three used to share one message that named
// none of them.
//
// The `z` unused-variable warning is declared, not designed away: the arm has to END in the
// declaration for the statement cause to be the one that fires, so the binding necessarily
// has no reader.
function f(c: boolean) { return if c { 1 } else { const z = 2 } }
print(f(true))
